# fr21_flag_conflicts.txtar — FR-21 / DR-001: mutually-exclusive flag
# combinations are rejected at flag-parse time, BEFORE any file read,
# with exit 1 and a canonical-shape stderr. STORY-0003 exercises the
# flag-pair combinations the STORY-0003 cobra tree actually parses:
#
#   - `--query` + `-f` (FR-13 acceptance)                   → flag.conflict
#
# Other FR-21 cells (`-i` + `--edit`, `--backup` without `-i`,
# `-n` + `--check`) are tested in later stories that introduce those
# flags (STORY-0004 for -i/--backup; STORY-0006 for --check/--dry-run;
# STORY-0007 for --edit). Each will land its own fr21_* fixture.

# --query + -f (FR-13) — the pair STORY-0003 CAN enforce.
#
# CRITICAL: this rejection MUST happen before any file read. We set the
# file to a nonexistent path and assert the error is the flag.conflict,
# NOT an IO error. That proves the check fires at flag-parse time.
! nesdit does-not-exist.json --query '.' -f q.jq
stderr '^nesdit: error: flag\.conflict: '
! stderr 'does-not-exist'
! stderr 'io\.read'
! stdout .

-- q.jq --
.z = 99
